"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+5 (ISUP grade group 5) and positive surgical margins, which are concerning features. Counterbalancing these are the absence of extraprostatic extension, no seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no lymph node metastasis. The biopsy findings showed a progression from Gleason 3+4 to Gleason 4+4.\n\nThe nodal status is documented as negative (no lymph node metastasis). The difference between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, with the prostatectomy grade being higher. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding future trajectory. Postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent changes are critical information that would revise this prognosis. The current horizon suggests a follow-up period without an event. The fixed estimated horizon is 40.43 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 30.32–50.53 months; its coverage has not been validated."
